{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789226303,"nonce":"I9YnM3GKA3eVPx3pwCrgck7L7L4=","title":"Tier modules keep compiling the pre-reload bytecode after a hot reload","message":"After a reload the interpreter clears the tiered entries and resets every bead, and it resolves bodies from the reloaded DecodedBytecode (`reloaded_bytecode`). The brokers do not: the tiered JITModule (and the Cranelift context) were built from the bytecode decoded at startup and keep it (`JITModule.bytecode`). A function promoted again after the reload is lowered from that snapshot, i.e. the OLD body, and installed over the new one.\n\nNot observed in scripts/test_hot_reload.sh because nothing gets re-promoted there: `getMessage` is declined by the demand gate and `main` stays interpreted. It reproduces in principle with a changed function that carries a hot loop and is called after the reload until the ladder promotes it again.\n\nWhat the reload path does today (346c461): perform_reload compiles the changed findexes in a fresh module with `reload_recompile` set (past the cost gate), and writes the addresses into both its own mirror and the live `module_ctx.functions_ptrs` table, which compiled callers load under hot reload. That covers compiled callers reaching the new body; it does not stop a later promotion from replacing it with the old one.\n\nFix shape: on reload, hand the brokers the new DecodedBytecode (swap the module's bytecode and invalidate every per-findex cache keyed on the old one -- func_cache, air cache is already invalidated, Cranelift's pinned snapshot), or retire the old brokers and start new ones on the reloaded bytecode.","files":null}]}